          "text": "I've now read every source file in the project. Here's the full picture, based purely on the code.\n\n# Project Zomboid Workshop Downloader — code analysis\n\n## What the application is\n\nA .NET 8 WPF desktop app for Windows x64 (only NuGet dependency: `Microsoft.Web.WebView2`). It is a Steam Workshop browser and mod manager for Project Zomboid (Steam AppId `108600`, hardcoded in `SteamCmdService.cs:16`). It lets the user browse the Steam Workshop in an embedded Chromium-based browser, download mods into a local PZ mods folder, track what's installed, check and apply updates, handle mod dependencies, uninstall/reinstall, and heavily customize the UI (themes, font sizes, list/tile views, thumbnails, tag filters).\n\n## Project structure\n\n| File(s) | Purpose |\n|---|---|\n| `App.xaml` | App-level resources: 7 theme brushes (`BgDark`, `BgPanel`, `BgField`, `FgText`, `AccentBlue`, `AccentGreen`, `BorderCol`), 4 UI font-size `sys:Double` resources, and full custom styles for Button/ToggleButton/TextBox/ComboBox/CheckBox/ProgressBar/ListBox/TabItem/DataGrid (hover/press animations, rounded borders, selection colors) |\n| `App.xaml.cs` | Global crash handling: hooks `DispatcherUnhandledException`, `AppDomain.UnhandledException`, and `TaskScheduler.UnobservedTaskException`; all three append to `%LOCALAPPDATA%\\PZWorkshopDownloader\\crash.log` and show a MessageBox. UI-thread exceptions are marked handled (app keeps running); unobserved task exceptions are marked observed |\n| `MainWindow.xaml` / `.cs` | The hub (~1470 lines of code-behind). Contains all five tabs, the window-level queue side panel, and the bottom status/log panel. Almost all feature logic lives here |\n| `Services/SteamCmdService.cs` | SteamCMD bootstrap + mod download/install, plus mod.info parsing helpers |\n| `Services/WorkshopApiService.cs` | Steam's key-less `GetPublishedFileDetails` API — item metadata (title, dates, content manifest id, creator) |\n| `Services/WorkshopVisualService.cs` | Same API, batched (100 ids/call) — fetches `preview_url` + tags |\n| `Services/WorkshopPageService.cs` | Scrapes the mod's HTML page on steamcommunity.com for its \"Required items\" section (regex-based, best-effort, browser-like User-Agent) |\n| `Services/ThumbnailStore.cs` | Local thumbnail cache under `%LOCALAPPDATA%\\PZWorkshopDownloader\\thumbnails\\<id>.jpg`, reused across runs |\n| `Services/SettingsService.cs` / `DownloadHistoryStore.cs` / `UninstalledModsStore.cs` | JSON persistence (plain `JsonSerializer`, best-effort save — failures are swallowed) |\n| `Models/` | Six small DTOs; `QueueItem`, `ModRecord`, `DownloadTask`, `UninstalledModEntry` implement `INotifyPropertyChanged` (needed because async pop-in of status/thumbnails updates rows in place) |\n| `DepsDialog.xaml(.cs)` | \"Show Dependencies\" popup with per-row Queue/Open Page buttons and an \"Add All to Queue\" action |\n| `AboutWindow.xaml(.cs)` | Version display + GitHub link placeholder (currently **not configured** — the button is disabled and it says so) |\n| `build.bat` / `run-debug.bat` | `dotnet restore + publish -c Release -r win-x64 --self-contained` into `dist/`; and `dotnet run` with a console kept open for startup errors |\n\n## How the pieces fit together\n\n`MainWindow` is the single orchestrator. It holds four `ObservableCollection`s (`_queue`, `_downloadedMods`, `_downloads`, `_uninstalledMods`) plus the in-memory log collection, and binds them to grids/tiles. The Services are all stateless statics that main-window code calls. All persistent state lives under `%LOCALAPPDATA%\\PZWorkshopDownloader\\`: `settings.json`, `download_history.json`, `uninstalled_mods.json`, `crash.log`, `steamcmd\\` (auto-bootstrapped), `thumbnails\\`. The app is single-threaded UI-wise; anything arriving on a background thread (SteamCMD output, async fetches) is marshaled back via `Dispatcher.Invoke`.\n\n**Central identifiability convention:** a mod is identified by its *numeric* Steam Workshop id, which lives in the numeric prefix of the installed folder name (`<workshopId> <DisplayName>`). The letter-based `id=` field in mod.info is deliberately **not** used for identification — only to resolve `require=` references against other installed mods (`TryExtractWorkshopId` at MainWindow.xaml.cs:480, with a mod.info numeric-id fallback scan).\n\n## Significant features\n\n**1. Embedded Workshop browser (Browser tab).** A WebView2 control opens on the trending PZ Workshop page. Back/Forward/Home buttons; the address bar turns any non-URL text into a Workshop *search* URL for appid 108600. Two actions: **Download This Mod/Collection** — extracts the numeric id from the page URL (`?id=N`) and the title from the document title (stripping the `::` suffix), then downloads it; and **Add To Queue**.\n\n**2. Download queue.** A window-level right-side panel that is auto-shown whenever the queue is non-empty (the grid column width animates 0↔280; MainWindow.xaml.cs:334). **Start Downloading All** processes items *sequentially* (this matters — `SteamCmdService` serializes all SteamCMD runs with a `SemaphoreSlim(1)` because SteamCMD doesn't handle concurrent instances), updating each item's status Queued → Downloading… → Done/Failed.\n\n**3. The download pipeline** (`StartDownloadAsync`, MainWindow.xaml.cs:359). Requires a configured mods folder, then: creates a `DownloadTask` at the top of the Downloads list and persists history; kicks off a background thumbnail fetch so the tile shows up immediately; runs `SteamCmdService.DownloadModAsync`, streaming each SteamCMD output line onto the dispatcher (into the task's Detail column and the log panel at DEBUG level); on success calls `WorkshopApiService.GetDetailsAsync` to record the live `hcontent_file` (content manifest) as `LocalModVersions[id]` plus an `InstallDates[id]` timestamp — this is deliberately best-effort so being offline right after a download doesn't fail it.\n\n**4. SteamCMD mechanics** (`SteamCmdService.cs`). On first use it downloads `steamcmd.zip` from Steam's CDN (~5 MB), extracts it into the app-data folder, and runs a harmless `+quit` once to absorb SteamCMD's self-update quirks. Downloads use `+force_install_dir` (the steamcmd folder itself) + anonymous login + `+workshop_download_item 108600 <id>`, then *copies* the content out of Steam's cache layout (`content/<id>/mods/<subfolder>/`) into the user's mods folder as `<workshopId> <DisplayName>` — taking the display name from mod.info's `name=` (sanitized) because authors usually name the physical folder after their internal `id=`. One workshop item may contain several mod folders (collections), so one download can install multiple folders; items without a `mods/` subfolder are installed raw. A nonzero exit code is tolerated when files actually landed on disk (SteamCMD's self-update relaunch can clobber the exit code).\n\n**5. Downloads tab.** A history grid (thumbnail, status, mod, detail, started, finished) plus **Clear Finished** / **Clear All**, persisted across runs in `download_history.json`, capped at 300 entries (newest-first). Right-click context menu removes entries.\n\n**6. Installed Mods tab — the core loop** (`RefreshDownloadedModsAsync`, MainWindow.xaml.cs:621). One pass does *both* listing and update-checking:\n- Scans the mods folder, resolves each directory to a workshop id (numeric folder prefix; mod.info fallback; folders starting `_` like `_backups` are skipped).\n- Builds two local indexes — installed workshop-id set, and a normalized (`lowercase alphanumerics only`) identifier→workshop-id map from every installed mod's mod.info `id=`/`name=` fields.\n- Each mod's `require=` entries are resolved **purely locally** against those indexes — zero extra Steam calls for dependency checking. Unresolved entries drive the \"Requirements\" column (`No Requirements` / `Requirements Met` / `N missing`).\n- Fetches live Steam details per mod (title, posted/updated dates, manifest, creator); failures (offline/removed items) are tolerated — the row still shows, by folder name.\n- **Update detection** compares the recorded local `hcontent_file` manifest against Steam's live one. The manifest id (not `time_updated`) is the version, because Steam's timestamp shifts on description edits too. Never-tracked mods (placed manually) are flagged as needing an update; unreachable Steam is *not* flagged (no false positives).\n- Computes local folder size from disk.\n\nEvery row also gets inline action buttons (open folder, open workshop page, **Update** — visible only when `NeedsUpdate` —, Force Redownload, Uninstall, Show Deps) plus a right-click context menu, and the grid has a pulsing orange status/requirements cell (implemented as an *Opacity* animation — animating `Brush.Color` would throw because WPF freezes brushes inside shared DataTemplates).\n\n**7. Visual subsystem (thumbnails + tags + tag filter).** After each refresh, a background pipeline (`LoadInstalledVisualsAsync`, MainWindow.xaml.cs:736) fetches all installed mods' `preview_url` + tags in batched API calls (100 ids per request), then downloads missing thumbnails with a `SemaphoreSlim(4)` gate so hundreds of mods don't hammer the CDN. Placeholders show the mod's initial letter until the image arrives, then the `ThumbnailPath` INPC event swaps it in — grid and tiles update in place. The **tag filter sidebar** (collapsible) is rebuilt from the union of tags across installed mods, preserving currently-checked filters; checking tags filters the shared `CollectionViewSource`, so the list grid and the tile view filter together (mods with no tag data stay visible). The same pipeline runs for the Downloads and Uninstalled lists at startup, and per-new-download.\n\n**8. List/Tile display + sizing.** Each of the three mod tabs (Downloads, Installed, Uninstalled) has a List/Tiles toggle; tiles are thumbnail + name + status text in a `WrapPanel`. A shared tile-size slider (80–260 px) writes the app-level `ModTileSize` resource live, resizing every tile in all three views at once.\n\n**9. Updating / uninstalling.** Per-row **Update This Mod**, **Force Redownload**, and **Update All** all funnel into `ForceRedownloadAsync`: if the *Backup updated mods* option is on, the folder is copied into `_backups/<folder>/<timestamp>`; the old folder is deleted first — necessary because SteamCMD only overwrites a folder whose name matches exactly, and a renamed mod would otherwise leave a duplicate; then it re-downloads and refreshes. **Uninstall** confirms, deletes the folder, and records the mod in the Uninstalled Mods list (persistent). The **Uninstalled Mods tab** shows those entries with **Reinstall** (fresh download; removes the entry on success), Open Page, and **Forget**, again with list/tile views.\n\n**10. Dependency handling (Show Deps).** The per-row Show Deps button fetches the mod's *Steam Workshop page* and scrapes its \"Required items\" section into (workshop id, title) pairs — the page is the authoritative source. Each listed dependency is annotated Installed/Missing by matching its id against the installed set. If the page has no required-items section, it falls back to resolving the local mod.info `require=` entries against installed-mod identifiers. Duplicates are guarded: a per-session cache plus an in-flight set, and it only ever runs on explicit user action. The `DepsDialog` lists everything with per-row **Queue** (only for deps with a known numeric id) and **Open Page** (direct page, or a Steam text search when no id is known), plus **Add All to Queue**.\n\n**11. Mod list import/export.** **Export List** writes an XML file (`ModList/Mod` with Name, WorkshopID, Author, Link). **Import** reads such a file, adds non-installed, non-queued entries into the download queue, and reports how many were added vs. skipped — the way to transplant a mod list to another machine.\n\n**12. Settings tab.** Mods folder picker (a single unified folder used as both the \"PZ Folder\" and download destination); three option checkboxes — *Backup updated mods before overwriting*, *Auto-refresh mod list when switching to Installed Mods tab* (the tab-switch hook at MainWindow.xaml.cs:122), *Show debug-level messages* — all persisted immediately; a **theme combobox with 10 themes** (Light, Dark, Midnight, Forest, Nord, Dracula, Solarized Dark/Light, Gruvbox, Monokai); and **four font-size sliders** (general/buttons tab headers/log, 10–20 / 8–18 range) plus a reset-to-defaults button.\n\n**How theming works** (`ApplyTheme`, MainWindow.xaml.cs:1417): each theme is a 7-color palette applied by *replacing* the app-level brush resources wholesale — WPF freezes purely XAML-declared `Freezable` resources for performance, so mutating a brush's `Color` in place silently fails; replacement is picked up immediately by every `DynamicResource` consumer. Font sliders work the same way by overwriting the app-level `UIFontSize*` `sys:Double` resources, which all control styles reference via `DynamicResource`.\n\n**13. Log panel.** Collapsible bottom panel (220 ms height animation). `AppendLog` is dispatcher-safe from any thread, prepends `[HH:mm:ss] [LEVEL]`, caps at 500 entries, and auto-scrolls only when the user was already at the bottom — otherwise a \"Jump to Bottom\" button appears instead of yanking the view. DEBUG-level lines (raw SteamCMD chatter) are suppressed unless debug logging is enabled. A status text line and an indeterminate progress bar accompany it.\n\n**14. Resilience by design.** Nearly every I/O path degrades gracefully: corrupt/missing JSON stores fall back to empty; persistence failures are swallowed; removed/invalid workshop items still produce a row; thumbnail failures never disturb a download; the deps scrape failing just disables \"Add All\" and shows a banner; theming is wrapped so it can't crash the app; and `App.xaml.cs` keeps the app alive even on unhandled UI exceptions, always leaving a `crash.log`.\n\n## One discrepancy worth flagging\n\nThe Settings tab help text (MainWindow.xaml:772) promises \"Scroll speed multiplies the mouse-wheel step of the tables and lists\" — but there is **no scroll-speed slider, setting field, or MouseWheel handler anywhere in the XAML or code**. That feature appears to be advertised in UI copy but not actually implemented (it may be a leftover from the roadmap). Everything else described in the XAML does have a corresponding implementation.",
