          "text": "Let me plan out the three changes:\n\n1. **Settings button to clear cached Workshop mods**: Delete `%LOCALAPPDATA%\\PZWorkshopDownloader\\steamcmd\\workshop\\content\\108600\\` (only the cached mod contents, not SteamCMD itself). Confirm before deletion. Add to the Settings tab. A good location would be, say, a \"Maintenance\" section or near the Mods folder section. In reality this is a cache, not a mod. Add a button like \"Clear Mod Content Cache\" with a confirmation MessageBox. The path is `SteamCmdService.SteamCmdDir` + `steamapps\\workshop\\content\\108600`. Wait — let me check the actual path structure: SteamCmdService uses `SteamCmdDir = %LOCALAPPDATA%\\PZWorkshopDownloader\\steamcmd`, and `GetContentPath` = `SteamCmdDir/steamapps/workshop/content/AppId/workshopId`. So the user's path `...steamcmd\\workshop\\content\\108600` is slightly different — what the user says is `steamcmd\\workshop\\content\\108600`, but the code is `steamcmd\\steamapps\\workshop\\content\\108600`. The code is the source of truth. So the cached mods are at `Path.Combine(SteamCmdDir, \"steamapps\", \"workshop\", \"content\", AppId)`. Delete the contents of the per-appid content directory... no, safely and simply: delete the directory `steamapps\\workshop\\content\\108600` (which is exactly the cache of Workshop content for PZ, and does not include steamcmd.exe or any app data). Deleting that directory tree removes only the cached mods. This matches \"delete only the cached mod contents, not SteamCMD itself.\"\n\nShould I add a public helper like `ClearContentCacheAsync` to SteamCmdService? Or put the logic directly in MainWindow? Following the existing architecture, a static method on the service makes sense: `SteamCmdService.ClearModContentCache()` returns info about what was deleted. For consistency with DownloadModAsync I'll add an async method with logging. Keep it simple: a synchronous delete would also be fine, since file IO is fast. But deleting large directories could take a little time. For UI responsiveness I'll go with async: use `Directory.Delete` (recursing) on background threads... actually, the simplest and most robust approach: run the deletion on a background task (Task.Run) and report results via logging. Let me write it like this:\n\n```csharp\npublic static string GetContentCachePath() =>\n    Path.Combine(SteamCmdDir, \"steamapps\", \"workshop\", \"content\", AppId);\n\npublic static async Task<bool> ClearContentCacheAsync(Action<string> log)\n{\n    var path = GetContentCachePath();\n    log(\"Clearing SteamCMD workshop content cache...\");\n    await Task.Run(() =>\n    {\n        if (!Directory.Exists(path)) return;\n        long size = ...; // optional\n        Directory.Delete(path, recursive: true);\n    });\n    log(\"Workshop content cache cleared.\");\n    return true;\n}\n```\n\nOn the MainWindow side: `ClearCacheButton_Click` — confirm with MessageBox.Show(YesNo, Warning): \"Delete the cached Workshop mod files? This only clears the SteamCMD download cache at %LOCALAPPDATA%... . Your installed mods in the PZ mods folder are NOT affected.\" Then invoke the service. Also disable the button while running. The confirmation text should clarify which path will be deleted.\n\nLocation in the Settings tab: add a \"SteamCMD Cache\" section. Add a new section to the Settings tab in the XAML: a header \"SteamCMD Content Cache\", a description text, and a button \"Clear Cached Workshop Mods\". Place it after the Mods folder section, or at the end before About. I'll put it after the Options section and before the Theme section — or at the very end after the UI customization. I'll add it after the Mods folder section (since it's storage-related). Actually, the logical grouping is: \"Cache\" is a maintenance task. To avoid confusion between the Mods folder and the cache, I'll add it as its own section after the UI customization, near the end. Either works. Let me insert it after the Options section... let me keep it simple: insert the new section right before the \"About\" button at the end of the Settings StackPanel.\n\nAlso note: while SteamCMD is running, the content folder may be in use. The button is on the Settings tab, so downloads could run concurrently. The deletion may fail with IOException. I'll catch it and show an error message (\"SteamCMD may be running\"). No problem.\n\n2. **GitHub link in the About window**: Update `AboutWindow.xaml.cs`: `GitHubUrl = \"https://github.com/IDRdh7/PZWorkshopDownloader\"` and `GitHubConfigured = true`. That enables the button.\n\n3. **Show the tile size slider only in tile mode, hide it in list mode, and update immediately on toggle**. Three `*_View_Checked` handlers. Currently each Checked handler sets the visibility of the grid/tiles. The sliders: there are three tile size sliders in the XAML (one per tab: lines 234–236 in the Downloads tab, lines 352–354 in Installed, lines 626–628 in Uninstalled). None of them has an x:Name. I need to give each slider a name, and also name the \"Tile size:\" label, so I can hide both the label and the slider in list mode (hiding only the slider would leave an orphan label behind — hmm, the user said \"the tile size slider should only be shown when the interface is in tile mode.\" The label \"Tile size:\" is part of that control group. Hiding both the label and slider is the clean UX interpretation. The instruction says to hide the slider. Hiding the label too is reasonable and I think it matches the intent — otherwise an orphan label shows. I'll hide both the label and the slider. Is this \"restructuring\"? No, it's minimal.\n\nDefault state: the toggle button has IsChecked=\"True\" on the List toggle in the XAML (all three tabs default to List). During InitializeComponent the Checked event fires, but the handlers are guarded by `if (!IsLoaded) return;` — meaning the visibility of the slider/label won't be updated during load. So the slider will show in list mode at startup, until the user toggles. Hmm. The requirement: \"when List mode is active, hide the slider.\" At startup list mode is active (List toggle is checked by default), so the slider should be hidden from the start.\n\nOptions:\n- Set the slider/label's initial Visibility=\"Collapsed\" in XAML? But then when the user switches to Tiles mode, the Checked handler sets it to Visible. And if the user goes back to List mode, the Checked handler sets it to Collapsed. This works: initial XAML visibility for slider+label is Collapsed (since default mode is List), and the handler handles the rest.\n- Alternatively, also apply the visibility in the IsLoaded guard... but the handler early-returns during InitializeComponent. The cleanest thing is: explicitly set the initial Visibility in XAML (collapsed since default is List mode), and update in the view Checked handlers.\n\nWait, subtle point though: the `Checked` event fires the moment IsChecked=True is set during InitializeComponent. The handlers early-return. So the XAML default must be Collapsed for the slider/label. Good.\n\nSo in XAML: for each of the three sections add `x:Name=\"DownloadsTileSizeSlider\"` etc., with `Visibility=\"Collapsed\"` on the slider and label. The label: give it x:Name like `DownloadsTileSizeLabel`. Then in each `*View_Checked` handler:\n\n```csharp\nbool tiles = tb.Tag?.ToString() == \"Tiles\";\n...\nDownloadsListToggle.IsChecked = false; // existing\nDownloadsTilesToggle.IsChecked = false; // existing\nDownloadsGrid.Visibility = tiles ? Visibility.Collapsed : Visibility.Visible;\nDownloadsTiles.Visibility = tiles ? Visibility.Visible : Visibility.Collapsed;\nDownloadsTileSizeSlider.Visibility = tiles ? Visibility.Visible : Visibility.Collapsed;\nDownloadsTileSizeLabel.Visibility = tiles ? Visibility.Visible : Visibility.Collapsed;\n```\n\nThe tile toggle's Checked handler sets the list toggle's IsChecked = false, which doesn't fire anything (only Checked is wired). So the Tiles Checked handler runs, and I'll update