e — use a small `readonly struct`, or just a `bool` success flag with `out` parameters. The simplest: have `WithSteamCmdLockAsync` continue to return `T`, choose `T = int[]`, or make the action never throw (wrap everything in try/catch inside the lambda) and have `WithSteamCmd`'s catch return `default!` — then in the main window check `result != null`. But the result type of `WithSteamCmdLockAsync` is… let me keep it generic while choosing `T` to be a reference type: e.g., return `ClearedCacheInfo?`… hmm, or simpler: have the lambda never throw (fully internal catches) — then `WithSteamCmd`'s catch is unreachable in this call path, and `result` will always be a non-null tuple value. But tuple structs can't be null.\n\nOK, the simplest correct design: return a reference-type DTO. A small `class CacheClearResult { public int Folders; public long Files; }` nested inside `SteamCmdService`? Add a nested class — trivial. Or just check `Result.Folders == 0 && Result.Files == 0` as an \"already empty\" signal? This is also confused: it's legitimate for the root to have directories but all deletions to fail (a permission error on all of them) → `deleted=0, files=0`, but that's also \"nothing deleted due to an error\", not \"already empty\"! For example, if all folders are locked by games/SteamCMD, `deleted=0` and `files` can also be `0` → I'd be reporting \"cleared: 0 folders\" as success. Well, acceptable? I already log per-folder deletion failures, so the user gets the warning; reporting \"cleared 0 folders, 0 files\" is technically accurate. The `Directory.Exists` pre-check handles the \"cache never existed\" case. So the `Equals(default)` failure only matters if `WithSteamCmd`'s catch actually fires — and that only happens if the lambda throws. So I just need to make sure the lambda doesn't throw: wrap `GetDirectories` in a try, and enumerate files with all the catches (already wrapped per file). Also `Path.GetFileName(dir)` is fine.\n\nPlan: inside the lambda, wrap `Get